              "bbox": [
                0.381,
                0.16,
                0.796,
                0.363
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.455,
                0.375,
                0.724,
                0.39
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 11.7 Resistors in parallel"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.42,
            0.553,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.6.1 Resistors in Series"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.448,
            0.901,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What happens to the value of current when a number of resistors are connected in series in a circuit? What would be their equivalent resistance? Let us try to understand these with the help of the following activities.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.542,
            0.484,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 11.4"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.586,
            0.876,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Join three resistors of different values in series. Connect them with a battery, an ammeter and a plug key, as shown in Fig. 11.6. You may use the resistors of values like 1",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Ω"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", 2",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Ω"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", 3",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Ω "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "etc., and a battery of 6 V for performing this Activity.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.661,
            0.671,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Plug the key. Note the ammeter reading.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.682,
            0.875,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Change the position of ammeter to anywhere in between the resistors. Note the ammeter reading each time.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.722,
            0.874,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you find any change in the value of current through the ammeter?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.787,
            0.901,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You will observe that the value of the current in the ammeter is the same, independent of its position in the electric circuit. It means that in a series combination of resistors the current is the same in every part of the circuit or the same current through each resistor.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
